<div class="principles-strip"><div class="container principles"><span><b>01</b> Start with a running app</span><span><b>02</b> Reuse the shared features</span><span><b>03</b> Own the experience</span><a href="#architecture">See how it fits together <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div></div>

<section id="quickstart" class="section container" aria-labelledby="quickstart-title">
  <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 / MAKE IT RUN</p><h2 id="quickstart-title">From fork to first login.</h2></div><p>Docker handles the application and its services.<br />You bring a name and an idea.</p></div>
  <div class="start-grid">
    <ol class="setup-steps">
      <li><span class="step-number">1</span><div><h3>Create your own starting point</h3><p>Fork the repository into your account, clone <strong>your fork</strong>, and open its root directory. Keep <code>README.md</code> and <code>AGENTS.md</code> close by.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/osogroup-blair/oso_mvc_boilerplate/fork" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fork the upstream repository ↗</a></div></li>
      <li><span class="step-number">2</span><div><h3>Bring the stack online</h3><p>Install Docker with Compose v2, then run the command below. First launch builds the app, applies migrations and seeds local demo data. Wait for the health checks to finish.</p><div class="code-block light-code"><div class="code-caption"><span>TERMINAL · YOUR FORK ROOT</span><button class="copy-button" type="button" data-copy="startup-command" aria-label="Copy Docker startup command" hidden>Copy</button></div><pre><code id="startup-command">docker compose up --build -d --wait</code></pre></div><details class="small-details"><summary>Need different ports?</summary><p>Use the same values on later Compose commands. Update the <code>PUBLIC_APP_URL</code> site setting for task email links.</p><pre><code>BOILERPLATE_HTTP_PORT=3100 \
BOILERPLATE_TEMPORAL_UI_PORT=8344 \
docker compose up --build -d --wait</code></pre></details></div></li>
      <li><span class="step-number">3</span><div><h3>Sign in. Take a look around.</h3><p>Open <a href="/admin">the admin area</a>. A freshly seeded local demo includes <code>alice@example.com</code> with password <code>password123</code>. Create your own accounts as you make the project yours.</p><div class="setup-links"><a href="/admin">Open admin ↗</a><a href="/health">Check app health ↗</a><a href="/pages/welcome">See a CMS page ↗</a></div></div></li>
    </ol>
    <aside class="setup-aside" aria-label="Local environment reference"><div class="aside-label">YOUR LOCAL WORKSPACE</div><dl class="local-addresses"><div><dt>Web application</dt><dd><code>localhost:3000</code></dd></div><div><dt>Temporal UI</dt><dd><code>localhost:8244</code></dd></div><div><dt>Database &amp; Redis</dt><dd>On the internal Docker network</dd></div></dl><p class="muted small">These are the default Docker ports. Custom ports use the values you set at startup.</p><div class="aside-rule"></div><h3>The commands you’ll use</h3><div class="command-reference"><code>docker compose ps</code><span>Check service health</span><code>docker compose logs -f web worker</code><span>Follow application and workflow logs</span><code>docker compose down</code><span>Stop while preserving your data</span><code>docker compose up -d --wait</code><span>Start the existing stack again</span></div><p class="aside-footnote">Database state and public/private uploads live in named volumes. Rebuild after code changes; ordinary restarts do not reapply demo seeds.</p></aside>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="features" class="toolkit-section" aria-labelledby="features-title"><div class="container section">
  <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / KNOW WHAT YOU HAVE</p><h2 id="features-title">A toolkit, already connected.</h2></div><p>Explore a feature, try its screen, then open the<br />implementation notes to see where to extend it.</p></div>
  <div class="feature-toolbar" hidden data-feature-toolbar><div class="filter-buttons" role="group" aria-label="Filter features"><button type="button" data-filter="all" aria-pressed="true">All features <span>12</span></button><button type="button" data-filter="build" aria-pressed="false">Build interfaces</button><button type="button" data-filter="automate" aria-pressed="false">Automate work</button><button type="button" data-filter="operate" aria-pressed="false">Operate</button></div><label class="feature-search"><span class="search-icon" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span><span class="sr-only">Search features</span><input type="search" id="feature-search" placeholder="Find a feature…" autocomplete="off" /></label></div>
  <div class="feature-grid" id="feature-grid">{{#each features}}<article class="feature-card" data-feature data-category="{{category}}"><div class="feature-card-top"><span class="feature-number" aria-hidden="true">{{number}}</span><span class="feature-category">{{categoryLabel}}</span></div><h3>{{title}}</h3><p>{{description}}</p><a class="feature-link" href="{{href}}">{{link}} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><details class="feature-details"><summary>Implementation notes <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><ol>{{#each steps}}<li>{{this}}</li>{{/each}}</ol><div class="file-list"><span>START IN YOUR FORK</span>{{#each files}}<code>{{this}}</code>{{/each}}</div></details></article>{{/each}}</div>
  <p id="no-features" class="empty-features" hidden>No matching features. Try “task”, “file” or “settings”, or choose another category.</p><p id="feature-count" class="feature-count" aria-live="polite" hidden>12 features to build on</p>
</div></section>

<section id="architecture" class="architecture-section container" aria-labelledby="architecture-title"><div><p class="eyebrow">ONE WAY THROUGH THE LAYERS</p><h2 id="architecture-title">Predictable by design.</h2><p class="muted">Follow the same path for your first feature and your fiftieth.</p></div><ol class="layer-flow" aria-label="Request lifecycle"><li><strong>Route</strong><span>Map the URL</span></li><li><strong>Controller</strong><span>Validate the request</span></li><li><strong>Service</strong><span>Apply policy</span></li><li><strong>Model</strong><span>Own the query</span></li><li><strong>Prisma</strong><span>Persist the data</span></li></ol><p class="architecture-caption">Views receive data. Activities call services. Workflow code stays deterministic.</p></section>

<section id="build" class="section container" aria-labelledby="build-title"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / BUILD SOMETHING YOURS</p><h2 id="build-title">Your first feature, end to end.</h2></div><p>Let’s say your app needs <strong>Customers</strong>.<br />Build one cohesive slice through the stack.</p></div><div class="build-grid"><div class="build-steps">{{#each buildSteps}}<details class="build-step" {{#if @first}}open{{/if}}><summary><span class="build-step-number">{{number}}</span><span>{{title}}<code>{{file}}</code></span><span class="expand-mark" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="build-step-body"><p>{{detail}}</p><span class="skill-reference">Read in your fork: <code>{{skill}}</code></span></div></details>{{/each}}</div><aside class="build-aside"><span class="aside-label">WORK WITH YOUR CODING AGENT</span><h3>Give it the architecture.<br />Then give it the goal.</h3><p>Start with <code>AGENTS.md</code>, then <code>ARCHITECTURE.md</code> and the relevant skill. Keep your domain’s rules in its services.</p><div class="code-block agent-prompt"><div class="code-caption"><span>EXAMPLE PROMPT</span><button class="copy-button" type="button" data-copy="agent-prompt" aria-label="Copy example coding agent prompt" hidden>Copy</button></div><pre><code id="agent-prompt">Read AGENTS.md, ARCHITECTURE.md,
and the relevant skills first.

Add an organization-scoped
Customer resource with name,
email and status.

Plan each layer. Reuse the admin
adapter. Check current access,
validate input, preserve command
identity and audit safe metadata.

Test cross-org denials, retries
and stale updates. Run typecheck,
lint and the architecture checks.</code></pre></div><p class="small muted">The agent follows the same contracts as a human contributor. Review its changes and test the behavior.</p></aside></div>
  <details class="runtime-note docker-edit-note"><summary>Editing and migrating a Docker-based fork</summary><p>Edit files in your local checkout, then rebuild the containers to run the new code. The default image runs compiled source. For a schema change, save the migration back into your checkout with this Bash/WSL command while the stack is running:</p><div class="code-block"><div class="code-caption"><span>BASH / WSL · AFTER EDITING PRISMA/SCHEMA.PRISMA</span><button class="copy-button" type="button" data-copy="migration-command" aria-label="Copy Docker migration commands" hidden>Copy</button></div><pre><code id="migration-command">docker compose run --rm --no-deps \
  -v "$(pwd)/prisma:/app/prisma" \
  initialize npx prisma migrate dev --name add_customer
docker compose up --build -d --wait</code></pre></div><p>Review and commit the new migration with your source. Use the host development setup in README.md when you want watchers and host tooling.</p></details>
  <div class="verify-band"><div><h3>Make the checks part of the feature.</h3><p>For host tooling, install Node 24, then run <code>npm ci</code> and <code>npx prisma generate</code>. Unit tests cannot access the database; runtime tests use owned fixtures.</p></div><div class="code-block"><div class="code-caption"><span>HOST DEVELOPMENT · NODE 24</span><button class="copy-button" type="button" data-copy="verify-command" aria-label="Copy verification commands" hidden>Copy</button></div><pre><code id="verify-command">npm run typecheck && npm run lint
npm run check:architecture && npm test</code></pre></div></div>
  <details class="runtime-note"><summary>Running the full local acceptance suite</summary><p>Provision the explicit PostgreSQL, Redis and Temporal fixture images plus Playwright/Chromium, then run <code>npm run test:local:all</code>. After building the Docker image, <code>npm run test:docker</code> checks a fresh isolated stack and persistence across restarts. See <code>docs/blueprint/LOCAL-OPERATIONS.md</code> for prerequisites and ownership rules. These are development checks, not production release approval.</p></details>
</section>

<section id="make-it-yours" class="finish-section"><div class="container finish-grid"><div class="finish-copy"><p class="eyebrow">04 / MAKE THE HANDOFF TO YOUR APP</p><h2>A small checklist.<br />A proper starting point.</h2><p>Use this guide until the foundation feels familiar. Then make your own product the front door.</p><div class="progress-area" hidden data-progress><div class="progress-label"><span id="progress-text">0 of 6 complete</span><button type="button" class="reset-progress">Reset</button></div><div class="progress-track" role="progressbar" aria-label="Implementation checklist progress" aria-valuemin="0" aria-valuemax="6" aria-valuenow="0"><span id="progress-fill"></span></div><span class="progress-note">Saved only in this browser.</span></div><p class="homepage-note"><strong>When you’re ready:</strong> publish your own CMS page and select it in <a href="/admin/settings">Settings → Identity → Home Page</a>. Customize or unmount this guide before a public launch.</p></div><div class="checklist">{{#each checklist}}<label class="checklist-item"><input type="checkbox" data-check="{{id}}" /><span><strong>{{label}}</strong><span>{{detail}}</span></span></label>{{/each}}</div></div></section>
